<article class="env-example" id="06S4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06S4">Example <span data-tag="06S4">90.3.7</span>.</a> Let $p$ be a prime number and let $n \in \mathbf{N}$. Let $\Lambda = \mathbf{F}_ p(t_1, t_2, \ldots , t_ n)$ and let $k = \mathbf{F}_ p(x_1, \ldots , x_ n)$ with map $\Lambda \to k$ given by $t_ i \mapsto x_ i^ p$. Let $A = k[\epsilon ] = k[x]/(x^2)$. Then $A$ is an object of $\mathcal{C}_\Lambda $. Suppose that $D : k \to k$ is a derivation of $k$ over $\Lambda $, for example $D = \partial /\partial x_ i$. Then the map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  f_ D : k \longrightarrow k[\epsilon ], \quad a \mapsto a + D(a)\epsilon  \]
</div>
  <p> is a morphism of $\mathcal{C}_\Lambda $. Set $A_1 = A_2 = k$ and set $f_1 = f_{\partial /\partial x_1}$ and $f_2(a) = a$. Then $A_1 \times _ A A_2 = \{ a \in k \mid \partial /\partial x_1(a) = 0\} $ which does not surject onto $k$. Hence the fibre product isn't an object of $\mathcal{C}_\Lambda $. </p>
</article>